  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe use self-similar ripple patterns to construct a piecewise-constant function h that minimizes the maximum overlap with its complement. We build upon the Erd\u0151s construction to create a more efficient function by embodying regular geometric structure in the time domain. Unlike the provided parent which is 45-point, our approach generates a candidate that better adapts to the cyclic correlation structure. We focus on reducing the correlation peaks through five distinct ripples with controlled oscillations in the overlap function. Our core idea is to leverage multi-scale ripple constructs whose frequency and phase are related to the domain size.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nWe define h on a grid of n_points points as a piecewise constant function with values in [0,1]. Our formulation uses a constrained optimization to maintain the integrated area exactly as 1:\n  - Variables: h[0], h[1], ..., h[n_points-1] \u2208 [0,1]\n  - Constraint: \u2211h[i] * dx = 1 (where dx=2/n_points)\n  - Objective: minimize max_k { dx * correlation(h, (1-h), mode='full')[k] }\n\nThe correlation is evaluated over all possible lags between -n_points+1 and n_points-1.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\na) Initialization:\n   - Start from 45-point Erd\u0151s construction (global variable initial_h_values provided)\n   - If n_points=45, we use the parent; otherwise, interpolate for higher resolution\n   - Ensure all values satisfy 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)*dx=1\n\nb) Multi-scale ripple construction (for n_points=45):\n   - We use a five-wave pattern with the form:\n        h(x) = A + B * \u220f(1 - (x+k_j)/L) for k_j in [0, 2] with scaling\n   - Applied after appropriate scaling and normalization\n   - Optimization parameters: number of ripples (5), attenuation factor (B)\n\nc) Local search refinement:\n   - Repair mechanism to adjust sum to target when needed\n   - Simplex search around the multi-scale candidate to explore better solutions\n   - Adaptive truncation to avoid sparse or unstable regions\n\nd) Transition for increased grid size (to n=90, then 90):\n   - Upsampling by linear interpolation (if n=45->90)\n   - Quadratic extrapolation (if n=90->180) with damping to avoid instability\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Starting point variations:\n        Version A: 45-point base\n        Version B: 90-point version derived from parent via interpolation\n        Version C: 180-point using quadratic extrapolation\n   - Numerical parameters:\n        * Ripple count: 3 to 7 (trial values: 5)\n        * Attenuation factor: [0.8, 0.95]\n        * Regularization strength: [0.0001, 0.0015, 0.005]\n\n5. Computer budget and robustness:\n   - Initial evaluation time: ~ (n_points)^2 operations per candidate grid\n   - Total search time estimate: 400 calls to objective function, scheduled for ~1100 seconds\n   - Fallback mechanism: Return the best of 45, 90, 180 point versions if optimization finishes low quality candidates\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Check that 0<=h[i]<=1 and \u2211h*dx \u2248 1 (within floating-point tolerance) using pure-Numpy repair\n   - Re-evaluate the candidate score before returning\n   - Retention allowances for time remaining during optimization\n   - Progress tracking and strategic jump capability based on score plateau detection",
